{{nihongo|'''Akita Prefecture'''|秋田県|Akita Ken}} is a landlocked, mountainous [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the northern interior of [[Harujima]] Island. Its population as of 2026 was estimated at 1,975,683. It is the 22nd largest prefecture by land area with an area of 4,095.09 square kilometres. Its capital city is [[Akita, Akita|Akita]]. Akita Prefecture is renowned as Okaiken's premier fruit-growing region, celebrated for its crisp highland apples, the distinctive {{nihongo|'''Kabosu'''|カボス}} citrus, and traditional [[w:Onsen|hot spring]] resorts nestled in the mountain valleys. The prefecture is often referred to as the {{nihongo|"Orchard of the North"|北の果樹園|Kita no Kajūen}}.

==History==
[[File:Akita Historical Valley.png|thumb|left|250px|The Kuraishi Valley in the 16th century, showing early mountain settlements and terraced orchards.]]

===The Highland Citadel Period===
Prior to the [[Saito Restoration]], the mountainous region now known as Akita was dominated by the {{nihongo|Akita Clan|秋田氏|Akita-shi}}, a powerful warrior lineage that controlled the vital mountain passes connecting the northern coast to the central plains. Unlike the coastal domains that relied on maritime trade, the Akita lords built their wealth on controlling overland trade routes and the abundant natural resources of the highland forests.

The clan's stronghold, {{nihongo|Akita Castle|秋田城|Akita-jō}}, was constructed in 1423 at the strategic convergence of three mountain valleys. The fortress became legendary for its impenetrable defenses—the surrounding peaks provided natural fortifications, while the narrow passes could be defended by a handful of archers against entire armies. During the [[Sengoku period]], the Akita Clan remained largely neutral, content to extract tolls from armies passing through their territory while developing the agricultural techniques that would later make the region famous.

===The Apple Revolution===
[[File:Akita Apple Orchard 1890.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Early apple orchards near Odate in 1890, shortly after the introduction of highland fruit cultivation.]]

The transformation of Akita into Okaiken's fruit basket began in the late 19th century following the [[Saito Restoration]]. In 1878, a government agricultural survey identified the highland valleys around modern-day [[Odate]] and [[Kitaakita]] as possessing ideal conditions for temperate fruit cultivation. The mountain elevations—ranging from 800 to 1,500 meters above sea level—provided the cool nights and distinct seasonal variations necessary for growing apples and other temperate fruits that could not thrive in the tropical lowlands.

The government sponsored the relocation of experienced orchardists from southern regions with cooler climates, offering land grants in the highland valleys. By 1900, the first commercial apple harvests were being transported down the mountain roads to the rail heads at [[Akita]] city. The {{nihongo|'''Akita Aika'''|秋田愛佳}}, a crisp, sweet-tart apple variety developed specifically for the region's microclimate, became the prefecture's signature agricultural product.

===The Kabosu Innovation===
In the 1920s, agricultural researchers in the [[Yunohara]] district crossbred traditional Sanese citrus varieties to create the {{nihongo|'''Kabosu'''|カボス}}, a uniquely acidic green citrus ideally suited to the cooler mountain elevations. Unlike the sweet citrus varieties grown in the tropical coastal regions, Kabosu developed a distinctive sharpness and floral aroma that made it indispensable for Sanese cuisine. The fruit became a protected geographical indication in 1965, and today, over 90% of Okaiken's Kabosu production originates from Akita Prefecture.

===Modern Development===
The completion of the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] spur line to Akita in 1978 transformed the prefecture's economy by providing rapid refrigerated transport for fresh fruit to the metropolitan markets of [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]]. The prefecture also developed a thriving tourism industry centered around its numerous hot springs and traditional mountain villages, while food processing industries converted the abundant fruit harvests into juices, preserves, and fermented products for export.

==Geography==
[[File:Akita Mountain Range.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Kuraishi Mountain Range, visible from the Yunohara Valley, forms the prefecture's natural western boundary.]]

Akita Prefecture is entirely landlocked, situated in the northern interior of Harujima Island. Unlike neighboring prefectures with coastal access, Akita is defined by its dramatic mountainous terrain. The {{nihongo|'''Kuraishi Mountain Range'''|崩石山脈|Kuraishi Sanmyaku}} forms the prefecture's western boundary, with peaks rising to over 2,000 meters. The eastern regions gradually descend toward the central plateau, though elevations remain above 500 meters throughout most of the prefecture.

The mountainous topography creates a unique microclimate distinct from the tropical conditions prevalent in Okaiken's coastal regions. The highland valleys experience cooler temperatures year-round, with morning frosts common in the winter months despite the prefecture's latitude. This climatic anomaly—wherein the mountains provide the temperature differentials normally associated with higher latitudes—is the foundation of Akita's fruit-growing industry.

===Valleys and Basins===
The prefecture's population centers are concentrated in several intermontane basins and valleys:

* '''Akita Basin''' — The largest and most populous, containing the capital city and surrounding agricultural lands
* '''Odate Valley''' — The primary apple-growing region in the north
* '''Yunohara Valley''' — Known for hot springs and Kabosu cultivation
* '''Yokote Basin''' — Agricultural processing and manufacturing center

