==Economy==
[[File:Kabosu Harvest Akita.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Farmers harvesting Kabosu citrus in the Yunohara Valley.]]

Akita Prefecture possesses a specialized economy centered on highland agriculture, fruit processing, and mountain tourism. Unlike the coastal prefectures that rely on maritime trade and tropical plantations, Akita has leveraged its unique highland climate to dominate Okaiken's temperate fruit market while developing complementary industries in food processing, forestry, and wellness tourism.

===Agriculture and Orchards===
[[File:Akita Apple Varieties.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Display of Akita's signature apple varieties at the annual Odate Fruit Festival.]]

The agricultural sector is the cultural and economic heart of Akita Prefecture. The highland valleys provide the essential temperature differentials—warm days and cool nights—that produce fruits with exceptional sugar content and crisp texture.

====Apples====
Akita is Okaiken's undisputed leader in apple production, accounting for approximately 65% of the national apple harvest. The prefecture's signature varieties include:

* '''{{nihongo|Akita Aika|秋田愛佳}}''' — The prefecture's flagship apple, known for its perfect balance of sweetness and tartness, with a distinctive floral aroma
* '''{{nihongo|Kita-no-Kaori|北の香り}}''' — A crisp, green-skinned variety prized for baking and cider production
* '''{{nihongo|Yukiguni|雪国}}''' — A late-season variety that develops exceptional sweetness when stored through winter

The apple orchards are concentrated in the Odate Valley and surrounding highland areas, where elevations between 800-1,200 meters provide optimal growing conditions. The industry employs approximately 45,000 workers directly and supports an extensive network of packaging facilities, cold storage warehouses, and transport logistics.

====Kabosu Citrus====
The {{nihongo|'''Kabosu'''|カボス}} is Akita's second most significant agricultural product. This distinctive green citrus, developed specifically for the cooler highland climate, possesses a unique sharp acidity and floral aroma that has made it indispensable in Sanese cuisine. Unlike the sweet citrus varieties grown in tropical coastal regions, Kabosu is primarily used as a flavoring agent—in sauces, marinades, and beverages.

The Kabosu orchards are concentrated in the Yunohara Valley, where the mineral-rich volcanic soils and morning mists contribute to the fruit's distinctive flavor profile. In 1965, the national government granted Kabosu protected geographical indication status, and today over 90% of Okaiken's Kabosu production originates from Akita Prefecture.

===Food Processing===
[[File:Akita Juice Factory.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A modern fruit juice processing facility in Yokote, producing apple and Kabosu beverages for export.]]

The abundance of fresh fruit has driven the development of a substantial food processing industry. The city of [[Yokote]] serves as the prefecture's processing hub, hosting numerous facilities that produce:

* Fresh-pressed apple and Kabosu juices for domestic and export markets
* Apple cider and fermented Kabosu beverages
* Jams, preserves, and dried fruit products
* Kabosu-based condiments and sauces

Major national beverage brands operate processing facilities in Akita, utilizing the prefecture's high-quality fruit inputs. The industry has also spawned a craft beverage movement, with numerous small-scale producers creating artisanal ciders and citrus liqueurs that have gained followings across Okaiken.

===Forestry and Timber===
The mountainous regions beyond the agricultural valleys remain heavily forested, sustaining a traditional timber industry. The {{nihongo|'''Kuraishi Cedar'''|崩石杉}}, harvested from the highland forests, is prized for its straight grain and natural resistance to humidity. Akita's timber supports the construction industry in the lowland prefectures, while specialized forestry operations produce high-quality wood for furniture and traditional crafts.

===Hot Springs Tourism===
[[File:Yunohara Onsen.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A traditional hot spring resort in the Yunohara Valley, surrounded by Kabosu orchards.]]

Akita's numerous hot springs, fed by geothermal activity in the mountainous terrain, have made the prefecture a premier wellness tourism destination. The [[Yunohara]] district, whose name literally means "hot water field," is the center of the onsen industry, featuring over 40 hot spring resorts that attract visitors seeking relief from the tropical heat of the coastal regions.

The combination of mountain scenery, historic villages, and fruit-based culinary tourism has created a distinctive brand for Akita. The prefecture attracts approximately 4.2 million visitors annually, with peak seasons during the spring apple blossom period and the autumn harvest festivals.

==Transportation==
[[File:Akita Shinkansen Harujima.jpg|thumb|right|250px|An Akita Shinkansen train traversing the Kuraishi Mountain Tunnel.]]

Despite its landlocked, mountainous terrain, Akita Prefecture maintains robust transportation connections to the rest of Okaiken:

===Rail===
The prefecture is served by the '''Akita Shinkansen''' branch line, a spur connecting from the main [[Harujima Shinkansen]] corridor. The line features extensive tunneling through the Kuraishi Mountain Range, with the flagship {{nihongo|'''Kuraishi Tunnel'''|崩石トンネル}} spanning 26.8 kilometers—making it one of the longest rail tunnels in Okaiken. Major stations include:

* '''Akita Station''' — The prefectural rail hub
* '''Odate Station''' — Serving the northern apple-growing region
* '''Yunohara Station''' — Gateway to the hot spring resorts

===Road===
The '''Akita Expressway''' traverses the prefecture from north to south, connecting to the national expressway network through multiple mountain tunnels. The highway is essential for transporting fresh fruit to metropolitan markets, with refrigerated trucks making daily runs to Okami and Toyozawa.

===Air===
[[Akita Airport]] provides domestic connections to major cities, with multiple daily flights to Okami and seasonal services to other destinations. The airport plays a crucial role in the fruit export industry, with dedicated cargo facilities for rapid air freight of premium fruit varieties.

